rows='''
foundations|Research and its quality|Research concept and nature|3|Research is systematic inquiry using evidence to answer questions or develop knowledge.|Good research is systematic, rigorous, empirical, critical, and sufficiently transparent to be checked. Appropriate control and valid measurement strengthen claims; perfect control is rarely possible in human settings.|Record classroom participation using defined rules, then analyse the pattern rather than relying on a teacher's impression.|Following steps neatly does not make a weak measurement valid.|A teacher says students seem more fluent. What would turn that impression into research?|Define fluency, formulate a focused question, collect suitable performance evidence, analyse it systematically and acknowledge alternative explanations.
foundations|Qualitative research|Qualitative research|5|Qualitative research interprets meanings, experiences and practices in context.|It commonly works with interviews, observations and texts. The researcher explains how evidence supports an interpretation, including cases that challenge it.|Interview quiet learners to understand how they experience speaking in class.|Qualitative does not mean unsystematic or simply non-numerical description.|Why might interviews help explain classroom silence better than a score alone?|Interviews can reveal meanings such as fear of peer judgment that a score does not capture. Accounts still need contextual analysis and do not directly prove actual behaviour.
foundations|Quantitative research|Quantitative research|5,28|Quantitative research measures variables and analyses numerical patterns, differences or relationships.|Specify what is measured, how scores are produced, and what comparison answers the question. Numerical precision does not compensate for a poor construct measure.|Compare fluency scores before and after a teaching intervention.|A quantitative study need not be an experiment.|Is counting the number of English words in student essays quantitative research?|It can be quantitative if the counts answer a clear question through systematic sampling and analysis. Counting alone is insufficient without a reasoned design and interpretation.
foundations|Mixed methods|Mixed methods|5,12|Mixed methods integrates qualitative and quantitative evidence in one investigation.|The strands may be sequential, concurrent, embedded or multiphase. Explain where they connect: design, sampling, analysis or interpretation.|Compare speaking scores, then interview learners to explain contrasting gains.|A questionnaire with an open comment box is not automatically a mixed-methods study.|You collect test scores and interviews but discuss them separately. What is missing?|A clear integration of the two strands. Explain how interview findings help interpret, challenge or extend the numerical results.
foundations|Research classifications|Research concept and nature|4,5,6|A study can receive different labels according to its purpose, objective, evidence, timing and design.|Basic research primarily develops knowledge; applied research addresses a practical problem. Exploratory, descriptive, correlational and explanatory labels concern the aim. These classifications can overlap.|A classroom experiment may be applied, quantitative, explanatory and longitudinal.|Basic research can have practical consequences; applied research can develop theory.|Can a study be both applied and qualitative?|Yes. Applied describes its practical purpose, while qualitative describes its approach to evidence and interpretation. They classify different aspects of the study.
foundations|Induction and deduction|Research concept and nature|5|Induction develops explanations from observations; deduction tests implications of existing explanations.|Inductive reasoning moves from cases to patterns. Deductive reasoning moves from theory through a prediction to evidence. Studies may alternate between them.|Develop a model of speaking anxiety from interviews, then test its predictions in another group.|A supported prediction does not prove a theory universally.|You start with a theory and predict higher fluency after practice. Which reasoning is this?|Deductive reasoning: the prediction follows from an existing explanation and is checked against data. Unexpected results may prompt inductive revision.
foundations|Longitudinal and cross-sectional designs|Research concept and nature|6,15|Longitudinal designs collect evidence over time; cross-sectional designs examine a population or groups at one time.|Panel designs repeatedly study the same individuals. Trend designs can use different samples from the same population; cohort designs focus on a group sharing a starting feature. Timing is separate from whether a study is experimental.|Follow the same learners across semesters to track vocabulary development, or compare year groups at one time for a cross-sectional snapshot.|A difference between year groups does not directly measure change within the same individuals.|Why might a panel design help investigate language development?|Repeated observations of the same learners can show individual change over time. Comparable measures and attention to attrition are needed; observed change does not by itself establish its cause.
methods|Narrative inquiry|Narrative inquiry|12,19|Narrative inquiry studies how people organise and interpret experience through stories.|Attend to sequence, turning points, identity and the setting in which a story is told. A story is a situated account rather than a transparent record of every event.|Ask a teacher to narrate becoming an English teacher and analyse how key events shape professional identity.|Narrative inquiry focuses on story construction, not merely extracting isolated themes.|How would you investigate a learner's changing relationship with English through narrative inquiry?|Invite a learning-history account, examine turning points and identity positions, and relate the telling to its social context. Preserve the sequence and the learner's meaning-making.
methods|Case study|Case study|17|A case study examines a bounded case in depth within its real context.|A case may be a person, class, programme or institution. Define boundaries of place, time and activity and use suitable evidence such as interviews, observations and documents.|Study one Grade 8 class's group work during one academic year.|A case is a bounded unit; it is not automatically a representative sample.|Why is 'students in Bangladesh' too vague as a case-study boundary?|It does not identify a specific bounded case. Specify a class, institution, group or programme and define its time, place and activity boundaries.
methods|Case-study types|Case study|17|Intrinsic studies examine a case for its own interest; instrumental studies use it to understand a wider issue.|Collective studies compare cases. Embedded studies examine subunits; holistic studies treat the case as one unit. Longitudinal refers to time and can combine with the other labels.|One school may be an instrumental case with three embedded classroom units.|These labels are based on different dimensions and are not mutually exclusive.|Can a case study be instrumental, embedded and longitudinal at once?|Yes. It can illuminate a broader issue, examine classroom subunits and follow the school over time. Each label describes a different feature.
methods|Action research|Action research|12|Action research is a practitioner's systematic cycle of investigating and improving local practice.|A typical cycle is plan, act, observe and reflect, followed by revision. The evidence may be qualitative, quantitative or mixed. Improvement claims need evidence rather than enthusiasm alone.|A teacher changes feedback routines, observes participation, reflects and adjusts the next lesson cycle.|Trying a new activity without systematic investigation is not enough.|A teacher introduces peer feedback. What makes this action research?|The teacher identifies a problem, plans a change, gathers evidence during implementation, reflects on the outcome and uses it to plan another cycle.
methods|Grounded theory|Grounded theory method|26,27|Grounded theory develops an explanatory theory or model through systematic analysis of data.|Coding and comparison support progressively developed categories and their relationships. Theory development is central; a list of themes alone does not establish grounded theory.|Develop a model of how new teachers adapt to unfamiliar classroom demands.|Thematic analysis and grounded theory can both code data, but their aims differ.|Why is an interview study with five themes not necessarily grounded theory?|Themes can describe patterns without producing an explanatory model. Grounded theory requires a coherent account of processes and relationships developed through systematic comparative analysis.
methods|Survey research|Survey research|15,16|Survey research systematically collects information about a population or sample's characteristics, behaviour or views.|Questionnaires and interviews are possible instruments. Plan the sample, pilot questions, collect and prepare responses, analyse and report the findings and limits.|Survey learners' reported weekly English practice and attitudes.|A survey is a design; a questionnaire is a tool within it.|Does a survey showing students like an app prove the app improves learning?|No. It measures reported preference or perception. Evidence of learning improvement requires an appropriate performance measure and design.
methods|Experimental and quasi-experimental research|Experimental research|6,13,14|An experiment manipulates an independent variable to examine an outcome; a true experiment uses random assignment.|A quasi-experiment studies an intervention without random allocation, often in intact classes. Comparison groups and baseline measures help address alternative explanations.|Randomly assign learners to two feedback conditions and compare writing outcomes.|Random sampling selects participants; random assignment allocates conditions.|Two existing classes receive different teaching methods. Is this a true experiment?|Usually it is quasi-experimental because learners were not randomly assigned. Initial class differences and teacher differences may influence the outcomes.
methods|Experimental designs|Experimental research|14|A pretest-posttest control-group design compares groups before and after an intervention.|Posttest-only designs avoid pretest sensitisation but lack direct individual gains. Repeated measures reuse participants; factorial designs examine multiple factors; Solomon designs examine pretest effects.|Measure fluency in both teaching groups before and after four weeks.|Improvement in one group alone is not evidence that its method caused the change.|Why include a comparison group when test scores improve after teaching?|It helps separate the intervention effect from maturation, test practice and other changes. Compare groups with suitable baseline and design controls.
methods|Corpus research|Corpus research|corpus|Corpus research studies language patterns in systematically assembled collections of texts or speech.|Searchable corpora support analyses of frequency, collocation and variation. Corpus selection must fit the research question; numerical patterns still require contextual interpretation.|Compare how often academic and conversational corpora use a target phrase.|A large collection is not automatically representative of all language use.|Why should raw word counts from differently sized corpora be normalised?|A larger corpus naturally contains more tokens. Rates per a common number of words permit a fairer frequency comparison, alongside attention to sampling and context.
methods|Digital discourse research|Digital discourse research|digital|Digital discourse research investigates communication mediated by digital platforms.|The research design considers platform features, participants, multimodal communication, data boundaries and ethical use. The same text can function differently across platforms and audiences.|Analyse how replies, emojis and quoted posts organise disagreement in a class discussion forum.|An online questionnaire is a digital collection tool, not automatically a study of digital discourse.|Why must a study of online disagreement consider platform design?|Features such as reply threading, visibility and character limits shape how messages are produced and interpreted. Analysis should connect linguistic choices with these technical and social conditions.
methods|Psycholinguistic methods|Psycholinguistics methods|psychomethod|Psycholinguistic methods investigate language processing through controlled tasks and behavioural or physiological measures.|Reaction times, accuracy and eye movements provide evidence about processing under specified task conditions. They require an explicit inference linking an observable measure to a mental process.|Compare reading times at an ambiguous phrase and a matched unambiguous phrase.|A slow response is evidence requiring interpretation, not a direct reading of a thought.|Why record both accuracy and response time in a lexical-decision task?|Response time can indicate processing difficulty, but faster responses may be less accurate. Considering both helps evaluate task performance and a possible speed–accuracy trade-off.
methods|Critical research|Critical research|26|Critical research explicitly examines power, inequality and the assumptions shaping knowledge or practice.|It asks whose interests are served and whose experiences are excluded. In language research, claims about ideology should be grounded in evidence and context, with attention to the researcher's own position.|Examine whose English varieties are treated as legitimate in classroom materials.|Critical does not simply mean finding faults or announcing a political conclusion.|How would a critical study examine an English-only classroom policy?|Investigate whose opportunities and identities the policy supports or restricts, analyse its language and implementation, and consider participants' experiences and institutional power.
methods|Interdisciplinary research|Interdisciplinary research|interdisciplinary|Interdisciplinary research combines concepts or methods from different fields to address a shared question.|The contribution comes from integration: perspectives should jointly explain the problem. Language learning can involve linguistic, psychological, social and educational processes.|Study speaking anxiety using psycholinguistic processing measures and sociolinguistic analysis of classroom identity.|Placing unrelated findings side by side is not strong integration.|How could linguistics and psychology jointly study oral participation?|Linguistic analysis can examine interaction patterns while psychological measures explore anxiety or processing demands. Integrate the evidence to explain when and why learners participate.
methods|Discourse analysis and CDA|Discourse analysis;Critical discourse analysis|26|Discourse analysis studies language in use; critical discourse analysis explicitly investigates power and ideology in discourse.|Both consider context and what communication does. CDA examines how inequality is sustained or challenged through representation, interaction and institutions.|Analyse who receives speaking turns; a CDA study additionally asks how those patterns reproduce authority or exclusion.|Ordinary discourse analysis is not restricted to sentence grammar.|How could two researchers analyse the same textbook differently using DA and CDA?|One may examine how explanations and tasks construct meanings. A critical analysis would explicitly investigate whose identities and interests are privileged or marginalised, linking textual evidence to social power.
methods|Ethnography and reflexivity|Case study;Data collection|18,19|Ethnography investigates a group's shared practices and meanings through sustained contextual engagement.|Participant observation, field notes, interviews and artefacts help connect participants' emic meanings with analytical perspectives. Reflexivity examines the researcher's influence and assumptions.|Follow a multilingual class over time to understand when code-switching signals solidarity.|One classroom visit is not a full ethnography.|What makes a description of code-switching 'thick'?|It explains the interaction's setting, participants, timing and social meaning, rather than simply recording that a language switch occurred.
basics|Research problem and scope|Statement of the problem|7|A research problem identifies a specific uncertainty or difficulty requiring investigation.|A problem statement sets the context, explains what is not adequately understood and defines boundaries. The four Ps—people, problems, programmes and phenomena—can help locate a manageable focus.|Narrow 'English speaking' to peer judgment and participation among first-year students in one department.|A broad topic or interesting title is not yet a research problem.|How would you narrow 'technology and English learning' into a researchable problem?|Select a population, setting, activity and uncertainty—for example, how first-year learners use mobile dictionaries during reading tasks—and check that evidence is accessible.
basics|Research objectives and justification|Objective of the study;Justification of the study|8,11|Objectives state what the study will achieve; justification explains why that achievement matters.|Use clear action verbs such as describe, examine or compare. Link each objective to a research question and explain its theoretical, practical or stakeholder relevance.|Objective: compare fluency gains. Justification: clarify whether the activity warrants classroom time.|Expected contribution is not a promise of positive results.|What is wrong with the objective 'to prove role-play is effective'?|It prejudges the outcome. Use an open, measurable objective such as comparing fluency outcomes under role-play and usual instruction.
basics|Research questions and hypotheses|Research questions and hypotheses|11|A research question asks what will be investigated; a hypothesis predicts a testable relationship or outcome.|Questions should be focused, researchable and aligned with obtainable evidence. A null hypothesis commonly specifies no population difference; an alternative specifies a difference. Exploratory studies may use questions without formal hypotheses.|Ask whether feedback type affects revision quality; predict greater improvement with one type.|A hypothesis is provisional, not an established fact.|Does every qualitative study need a formal hypothesis?|No. Exploratory qualitative research often uses open research questions to understand meanings and processes without a predetermined statistical prediction.
basics|Operational definitions and variables|Definition of terms|13|An operational definition states how a concept will be observed or measured in a particular study.|Distinguish the independent variable, outcome variable and possible confound. The operational measure must fit the construct and its limits must be acknowledged.|Define a fluency measure as words per minute during a comparable two-minute speaking task.|Words per minute measures one aspect of fluency, not all speaking ability.|Why is 'students improved their English' an inadequate operational definition?|It does not specify which ability, measurement task, scoring rule or comparison establishes improvement. Define an observable outcome linked to the research question.
basics|Limitations and confounds|Limitations of the study|13,21,30|Limitations are constraints on the strength, scope or interpretation of a study's conclusions.|Discuss sampling bias, measurement limits, confounding and contextual boundaries. A confound varies with the proposed cause and can influence the outcome, creating a competing explanation.|Only the experimental class has a highly experienced teacher.|A large sample cannot repair a biased selection method.|The new method's group has the best teacher. What threatens the conclusion?|Teacher experience is confounded with method. Differences could reflect teaching expertise rather than the method, so the design or conclusion must address that alternative.
literature|Literature review and synthesis|Literature review: what, why and how|8,9|A literature review critically synthesises relevant research to establish what is known, debated and missing.|Search, evaluate, compare themes and methods, organise the argument, then connect the review to the study. Thematic, chronological, methodological and theoretical structures are possible.|Compare surveys and observations of anxiety instead of listing one author after another.|An annotated bibliography describes individual sources; a review connects them.|How can a literature review justify a new study without claiming that nobody studied the topic?|Identify a specific unresolved issue supported by the reviewed evidence, such as limited observation of a particular population or disagreement between findings.
literature|Research gaps|Literature review: what, why and how|10|A research gap is an important absence, weakness or unresolved issue in existing knowledge.|The guide distinguishes evidence, knowledge, methodological, population, theoretical, empirical and practical-knowledge gaps. Categories can overlap, so explain the specific missing element.|Several studies use self-report only; observation could address a methodological gap.|A topic being new to you does not make it a research gap.|How does a population gap differ from a methodological gap?|A population gap concerns an underrepresented group. A methodological gap concerns limitations in how evidence was obtained or analysed; one study may address both.
evidence|Population, sample and sampling|Sampling|20,21|A population is the defined group of interest; a sample is the subset actually investigated.|Sampling specifies how units enter the study. Define the target population, accessible population, selection procedure and reasons for sample size.|Select 40 learners from a department of 400.|A sample is not automatically representative because it is large.|What is the difference between random selection and random assignment?|Selection chooses who enters the sample; assignment places sampled participants into study conditions. The former supports representativeness, while the latter helps reduce confounding.
evidence|Probability sampling|Sampling|20|Probability sampling gives each population member a known non-zero chance of selection.|Simple random sampling selects individuals; systematic sampling uses a random start and interval; stratified sampling draws within subgroups; cluster sampling selects natural groups.|Randomly select learners within each academic year for a stratified sample.|Probability sampling does not always give equal selection chances.|How do stratified and cluster sampling differ?|Stratified sampling draws from each relevant subgroup. Cluster sampling selects some natural groups, then studies all or sampled members within those groups.
evidence|Non-probability sampling|Sampling|20|Non-probability sampling selects cases without known random selection probabilities.|Convenience selects accessible participants; purposive selects relevant experience; snowball uses referrals; quota fills subgroup targets without random selection; maximum variation seeks diverse cases.|Recruit teachers with experience of multilingual classrooms for in-depth interviews.|Purposive selection can serve a qualitative goal without supporting statistical representativeness.|Why might purposive sampling fit an interview study better than convenience sampling?|The research may require participants with particular experiences. Purposeful selection aligns the evidence with that focus rather than relying only on whoever is easiest to reach.
evidence|Sample size and saturation|Sampling|21|Sample size should fit the research question, variation, analytical needs and available resources.|Quantitative studies consider precision and power; qualitative studies consider information richness and adequacy. Saturation refers to diminishing relevant new information, not a universal interview count.|A diverse population may require broader sampling than one narrow experiential group.|A fixed number such as 20 interviews does not guarantee saturation.|Why is 'I interviewed 20 people, therefore saturation occurred' weak reasoning?|The adequacy of the data depends on diversity, depth, scope and analysis. Explain what additional interviews contributed and how the relevant categories became sufficiently developed.
evidence|Questionnaires and instrumentation|Instrumentation|22,23|Instrumentation concerns the tools and procedures used to gather or measure evidence.|A questionnaire can include closed, open or mixed items. Use neutral, clear wording, one issue per item, defined timeframes and a pilot. Match the response format to the evidence needed.|Separate 'The app is easy and improves pronunciation' into two questions.|A self-reported confidence scale is not a direct test of speaking skill.|Why is 'How helpful is this excellent app?' a poor questionnaire item?|It leads the respondent through positive wording. A neutral version asks how helpful, if at all, the app was and allows negative or uncertain responses.
evidence|Validity and reliability|Instrumentation|3,16|Validity concerns the appropriateness of an interpretation; reliability concerns measurement consistency.|Use measures that fit the construct and procedures that minimise avoidable inconsistency. Training raters and piloting instruments can reveal weaknesses.|Two raters consistently apply a speaking rubric, but a vocabulary-only test would inadequately represent fluency.|Consistency alone does not establish validity.|Can an instrument be reliable but invalid for a purpose?|Yes. It may consistently measure the wrong construct—for example, a stable vocabulary score used as if it fully measured spontaneous speaking fluency.
evidence|Observation and interviews|Data collection|24,25|Observation records actions and interaction; interviews explore participants' accounts and interpretations.|Specify observation categories, participation, disclosure and setting. Interviews may be structured, semi-structured or unstructured; flexible probes help clarify meaning.|Observe participation and ask learners how they experienced the interaction.|What people report doing can differ from what is observed.|Why combine an interview with classroom observation?|Observation provides evidence of practice; interviews explain participants' meanings and experiences. Their differences can identify questions that either source alone would miss.
evidence|Triangulation|Triangulation|24|Triangulation examines a phenomenon using multiple evidence sources or approaches.|Comparing perspectives can strengthen an interpretation or expose contradictions. Disagreement should be investigated rather than hidden to produce apparent agreement.|Compare learner interviews, class recordings and teacher notes about participation.|Several methods do not automatically guarantee truth.|What should you do if interviews report high participation but observation shows little?|Examine the timeframe, definitions, observer effects and participants' interpretations. The disagreement is evidence to explain, not a reason to discard an inconvenient source.
evidence|Ethics and consent|Data collection|21,22,25|Research ethics requires informed, voluntary participation and appropriate protection of people and data.|Explain the tasks, uses, risks, withdrawal conditions and privacy arrangements. Confidentiality protects identifiable information; anonymity means identities are not known or recorded in the relevant process.|Explain recording use before an interview and avoid coercing students whose grades you control.|Researchers cannot guarantee what fellow focus-group members will disclose later.|Why is teacher-led research with the teacher's own students ethically sensitive?|The power relationship may make refusal feel unsafe. Participation and assessment should be separated, and students need a credible way to decline without disadvantage.
analysis|Research transcription|Data collection|27|Transcription represents recorded speech in written form using conventions appropriate to the research question.|Verbatim records retain relevant repetitions, pauses and false starts. Intelligent-verbatim and edited versions improve readability but may remove interactional evidence. Document decisions and check the record against the recording.|Preserve a pause and self-correction when investigating repair, because their placement can explain how the exchange develops.|A cleaned transcript is not a neutral copy: removing a feature can remove evidence.|Why might edited transcription be unsuitable for conversation analysis?|Editing can erase pauses, overlaps and repairs that show how turns and actions are organised. Preserve the details needed by the analysis and state the transcription conventions.
analysis|Qualitative coding|Data collection|26,27|Coding labels meaningful data segments; analysis develops relationships and interpretations from those labels.|Codes can form categories and themes. A theme should address a meaningful pattern relevant to the question, supported by extracts and checked against contrary cases.|Code 'I stay quiet because classmates laugh' as fear of peer judgment.|A frequent word or broad topic is not automatically an analytical theme.|What is the difference between the code 'anxiety' and a developed theme?|A code labels a segment or idea. A theme articulates a broader pattern, such as peer judgment limiting participation, and explains its relevance across the evidence.
analysis|Frequency and distributions|Frequency;Distribution|28,29|Frequency counts occurrences; a distribution shows how observations are spread across values or categories.|Tables and graphs reveal patterns, concentrations and variation. Percentages express counts relative to a total; check the denominator and missing data.|18 of 30 learners is 60 percent, provided all 30 supplied relevant responses.|A numeric code for a category is not a measured quantity.|If 18 of 30 students use a dictionary daily, what percentage is that?|It is 60 percent: 18 divided by 30, multiplied by 100. State the sample and what daily use means.
analysis|Central tendency and spread|Distribution|28,29|Mean, median and mode describe central tendency; range and standard deviation describe spread.|The mean uses all values, the median is the sorted middle, and the mode is most frequent. Standard deviation summarises dispersion around the mean.|For 2, 4, 4, 6: mean 4, median 4, mode 4 and range 4.|Two groups with equal means can have very different distributions.|Why report standard deviation alongside a mean speaking score?|The mean gives the centre but not how varied learners are. Standard deviation helps describe the spread and prevents equal means from hiding different patterns.
analysis|Chi-square|Chi-square|29|A chi-square test of independence assesses association between categorical variables using observed and expected counts.|The test compares counts with those expected under independence. Use a suitable contingency table and check expected-count and independence assumptions.|Test whether preferred feedback type is associated with year of study.|A significant association does not establish causation or its strength by itself.|Would chi-square normally compare two mean fluency scores?|No. A chi-square independence test is for categorical counts. A comparison of means requires an appropriate mean-comparison procedure and its assumptions.
analysis|Pearson correlation|Pearson correlation coefficient|pearson|Pearson's r measures the direction and strength of a linear relationship between two quantitative variables.|It ranges from minus one to plus one. Zero indicates no linear association, not necessarily no relationship. Outliers and nonlinearity can make a summary misleading.|A negative relationship could link higher anxiety scores with lower participation counts.|Correlation does not by itself show that one variable causes another.|If r is zero, must two variables be unrelated?|No. There may be a nonlinear relationship. Inspect the pattern and the measurement context instead of treating zero correlation as proof of complete independence.
analysis|Z-tests and t-tests|Zed test;T-test|ztest|A z-test uses the standard normal reference distribution; a t-test for a mean uses a t distribution when population variation is estimated.|For the classic one-sample mean z-test, the population standard deviation is known and other distributional conditions must hold. A t-test uses sample variation. Test selection is not determined by sample size alone.|Use a paired t-test to examine within-person pretest–posttest differences when its assumptions fit.|A sample above 30 does not automatically require a z-test.|Would you use an independent or paired t-test for the same learners' pretest and posttest scores?|A paired t-test treats each learner's two scores as a matched pair and analyses the differences, assuming the difference scores meet the required conditions.
analysis|Factor analysis|Factor analysis|factor|Factor analysis models shared patterns among observed variables using a smaller number of latent factors.|It can explore whether questionnaire items reflect underlying constructs. The number and interpretation of factors require evidence and theoretical judgment.|An anxiety questionnaire may contain related items about communication apprehension and evaluation fears.|A statistical factor is not automatically a proven psychological cause.|Why use factor analysis while developing a learner-attitude questionnaire?|To examine patterns of shared variation and whether items cluster into plausible underlying dimensions, while also considering theory and other validity evidence.
findings|Results and discussion|Presentation of findings;Discussion|30|Results present the evidence; discussion explains what the findings mean in relation to questions and prior research.|Use tables, figures, extracts and clear prose as appropriate. Interpretation should address alternative explanations and limits, not simply repeat the numbers.|Report an observed score change, then discuss its possible meaning and relation to earlier findings.|Qualitative findings are results too; results are not only statistical tables.|How does 'the mean increased by 15' differ from a discussion claim?|The first describes a result. Discussion explains its significance and possible reasons, compares it with prior work and considers whether the design supports a causal interpretation.
conclusion|Inference and recommendations|Inference;Recommendations|3,30|Inference draws conclusions from evidence; recommendations propose actions supported by those conclusions.|Keep claims within the design, sample and measurement limits. Recommendations can address practice or future research and should specify the context in which they apply.|A small local case may suggest testing a strategy in other settings before wider adoption.|A promising local result is not a universal teaching prescription.|Can a study of one class justify a policy for every university?|Not by itself. It can provide contextual insight and motivate broader investigation, but sweeping recommendations exceed the evidence's scope.
writing|Citation and plagiarism|Plagiarism;Citations;In-text references|31|Citation credits borrowed ideas and words; plagiarism presents another person's work as one's own.|A genuine paraphrase changes the structure and wording while retaining the meaning and citing the source. Exact wording requires quotation treatment and a locator.|After explaining an author's idea in your own words, include author and year and a matching reference.|Changing a few words does not turn copied structure into an adequate paraphrase.|Does paraphrasing remove the need for a citation?|No. The idea still comes from a source. Credit the author and year even when using your own wording.
writing|APA in-text citation and quotation|APA style;In-text references|31,32|APA uses author–date in-text citations linked to entries in a reference list.|Ordinary three-or-more-author in-text citations use the first surname and et al. Direct quotations need a page or other suitable locator. Quotations of 40 words or more use block format.|A fictional example is (Rahman et al., 2024); a quotation might add p. 12.|The reference list uses full author details under APA rules, not ordinary in-text et al. shortening.|What extra information does a direct quotation need compared with an ordinary paraphrase?|It needs quotation formatting and a specific locator such as a page number, as well as the author and year. A locator is optional but often useful for a paraphrase.
writing|APA reference entries|APA style|32|A reference entry identifies a source through its author, date, title and publication information.|Identify the source type first. Books, journal articles and chapters require different arrangements. APA 7 omits a publisher's city and presents available DOIs as URLs.|A chapter entry starts with its chapter author, then identifies the editor and book.|Do not invent missing dates, page ranges, journals or DOIs to fill a format.|Who appears first in an APA reference for a chapter in an edited book?|The chapter author. The editor appears later with the book information because the specific cited contribution is the chapter.
writing|Research proposals and frameworks|Writing a research proposal|30|A proposal explains what a study plans to investigate, why it matters and how it will be conducted.|Include the problem, literature, questions, framework, methods, ethics, expected contribution, timeline and references. A theoretical framework draws on theory; a conceptual framework specifies the study's concepts and relationships.|Plan a study of role-play and fluency with participants, measures, comparison and analysis.|A proposal must not present invented results as if the study has happened.|What should a proposal say about findings before data are collected?|It may state hypotheses or expected contributions, clearly as expectations. It must not fabricate findings; it should explain how the eventual evidence will answer the questions.
writing|Research papers and dissertations|Structures of research papers and dissertations;Writing a research article|30|A research report explains the question, methods, evidence and reasoned conclusions of a completed study.|A common dissertation structure includes introduction, literature review, methodology, results, discussion and conclusion. Articles often compress these functions; requirements vary by discipline and venue.|A report connects its discussion back to the research questions and ends with implications and limits.|An abstract summarises the whole study rather than serving as another general introduction.|What should a concise research-article abstract normally cover?|The problem or purpose, the main methods, central findings and conclusion or implication. For a proposal, describe planned work and omit invented findings.
'''
